e but not full compliance. Might be partial. I'd say 3.\n\nApplicability: degree idea clearly applies to stated problem. Solves identified problem directly related to stated problem (do X to get Y, Y is part of stated problem). Do X develop compressed logging to reduce overhead, Y is meet logging mandate with less cost. That's directly related. So 4 = solves identified problem directly related.\n\nReturn rating echo id.\n\nNeed format: For each subscale give \"<subscale>_evidence\" one sentence then \"<subscale>\" integer.\n\nProbably output JSON? Instruction says return rating echoing its id exactly. For each subscale... give ... first and then ...\n\nProbably produce lines.\n\nLet's produce:\n\nid: P2950\nimplementability_evidence: ...\nimplementability: 3\netc.\n\nEnsure one sentence evidence.\n\nMake sure sentences end with period.\n\nImplementability evidence: A small dedicated engineering team can experiment with asynchronous logging and data pruning within existing R&D resources, which is reasonably feasible. Score 3.\n\nAcceptability evidence: Developing efficient logging to comply with EU AI Act requirements is a common, legally aligned engineering practice that violates no norms. Score 4.\n\nOriginality evidence: Turning compliance overhead into a research track on compressed telemetry is unusual and shows imagination beyond routine logging implementation. Score 3.\n\nParadigm_relatedness evidence: The proposal weaves logging into scientific work and reframes compliance as an efficiency research opportunity, changing the lab's relationship to its obligations. Score 3.\n\nEffectiveness evidence: Reducing compute overhead from logging helps meet Article 12 without crippling performance, but it is a partial contribution rather than full guaranteed compliance. Score 3.\n\nApplicability evidence: Minimizing performance hit from continuous record-keeping directly addresses how the lab can meet the mandatory logging mandate. Score 4.\n\nReturn.\n\nLet's ensure or