ding mandate for high-risk AI projects in the lab.\n\nEffectiveness evidence: With uniform APIs embedded in CI/CD, all projects can automatically generate the logs required for traceability and post-market monitoring.\n\nOriginality evidence: Centralized logging middleware for regulatory compliance is a common, mundane software engineering solution.\n\nParadigm_relatedness evidence: It introduces new tools and processes but still treats compliance as conventional logging practice without changing the lab’s R&D-compliance relationship.\n\nImplementability evidence: Developing a small middleware library and integrating it into an existing shared CI/CD pipeline requires some engineering effort but is reasonably feasible.\n\nAcceptability evidence: Standardized compliance tooling is a common strategy that violates no legal, social or business norms.\n\nScores: applicability 4, effectiveness 4, originality 1, paradigm_relatedness 2, implementability 3, acceptability 4.\n\nCheck definitions: effectiveness 4 = reasonable and will solve stated problem without regard for workability (if you could do it, it would solve main problem). Yes.\n\nCould effectiveness be 3? It contributes to solution but maybe not full? Article 12 also requires specific content for point 1(a) systems. Framework may need customization. Hmm still solves logging mandate.\n\nKeep 4.\n\nReturn.\n\n",
