ess: degree will solve problem.\nWill it solve stated problem? It develops methods, but it's research project within internal testing environments, not guaranteed to meet mandate now. Reasonable and will contribute to solution of problem (helps but partial). Could be 3. If you could do it, would solve main problem? Might be partial because still R&D not deployed. Level 3 = reasonable and will contribute to solution... it helps but only partial solution. Yes.\n\nNovelty\noriginality: degree rare but ingenious...\nPrivacy-preserving traceability research project with ZK proofs for logging. Is that unusual interesting shows some imagination? Could be 3 = unusual, interesting; shows some imagination. Not expressed before AND ingenious? Might be 3 not 4. Probably 3.\n\nparadigm_relatedness: degree preserves or modifies paradigm.\nParadigm breaking: introduces new elements AND changes relationship between lab's R&D and compliance obligations, or addresses larger problem of what lab researches\nParadigm stretching: changes that relationship (e.g., logging woven into science, new partners or offerings born from compliance)\nSlightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements but still treats compliance as logging practice\nParadigm preserving: standard compliance...\n\nProposal transforms regulatory burden into publishable scientific contribution. That's changing relationship between R&D and compliance obligations. It weaves logging into science. That sounds like paradigm stretching (3) maybe paradigm breaking? Introduces new elements AND changes relationship... Could be 4 = paradigm breaking: introduces new elements AND changes relationship between lab's R&D and its compliance obligations, or addresses larger problem of what lab researches. This does change relationship: compliance becomes research topic. Might be paradigm breaking? The description: transforms regulatory burden into publishable scientific contribution that enhances trust. That's changing relationship. Could be 4. Hmm instrument says paradigm breaking introduces new elements AND changes relationship ... or addresses larger problem of what lab researches. This is about logging methods, not larger problem of what lab researches? It is R&D initiative.\n\nProbably 3 = paradigm stretching: changes that relationship (e.g., logging woven into the science...). Paradigm breaking would be maybe stop doing high-risk AI? Might be 3.\n\nLet's pick 3 for paradigm_relatedness.\n\nProvide output with id and each subscale evidence sentence then score.\n\nNeed format: echo id exactly. For each subscale, give \"<subscale>_evidence\" first then \"<subscale>\" integer.\n\nProbably JSON-like.\n\nReturn rating.\n\nMake sure one sentence evidence each.\n\nImplementability evidence: Developing zero-knowledge proofs and differentially private logging requires specialized cryptography expertise and extensive testing beyond routine compliance logging. Score 2.\n\nAcceptability evidence: Privacy-preserving logging aligns with data minimization norms and EU regulatory expectations, raising no legal or social objections. Score 4.\n\nApplicability evidence: The project directly targets the Article 12 mandate to record events for high-risk AI while addressing sensitive input data requirements. Score 4.\n\nEffectiveness evidence: Validating methods against Annex III could yield compliant logging, but as an internal research project it offers only a partial, future contribution rather than immediate mandate fulfillment. Score 3.\n\nOriginality evidence: Combining zero-knowledge proofs with AI audit logging for Annex III compliance is unusual and shows imaginative integration of privacy tech. Score 3.\n\nParadigm_relatedness evidence: The initiative reframes compliance logging as a core R&D theme, weaving logging into scientific work rather than treating it as mere administrative practice. Score 3.\n\nEnsure order listed: implementability, acceptability, applicability, effectiveness, originality, para