mine where Article 12 record-keeping is legally required. Score 4.\n\nparadigm_relatedness (1-4): degree to which idea preserves or modifies paradigm.\nOptions: 4 paradigm breaking introduces new elements AND changes relationship between lab's R&D and compliance obligations, or addresses larger problem of what lab researches. 3 paradigm stretching changes that relationship (e.g., logging woven into science, new partners...). 2 slightly paradigm stretching introduces new elements but still treats compliance as logging practice. 1 paradigm preserving standard compliance.\n\nAudit is standard practice? Classification review is fairly standard compliance scoping. Introduces legal-scientist collaboration monthly resource reviews - maybe slightly new elements but still treats compliance as logging practice. Might be 2 = slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements (tools, formats, processes) but still treats compliance as logging practice. Could be 1 paradigm preserving: standard compliance and logging practice; lab's way unchanged. Hmm.\n\nThe proposal is a comprehensive review to determine which fall under high-risk. That's fairly standard risk classification. I'd say paradigm preserving? It doesn't change relationship between R&D and compliance obligations, just identifies scope. It's standard. Maybe 1.\n\nLet's pick 2? The text says legal team will collaborate with lead scientists during monthly resource reviews. That's a process change but not radical. Paradigm_relatedness 1 = paradigm preserving: standard compliance and logging practice; lab's way of working is unchanged. The audit is typical. I'd say 1.\n\nEvidence: The proposal uses conventional risk-mapping to limit logging scope without altering how R&D is conducted or integrating compliance into research design. Score 1.\n\noriginality (1-4): degree to which idea is rare but ingenious...\nCommon, mundane, boring =1. Classification audit is common. So 1.\n\nEvidence: Risk classification audits against Annex III are a common, expected first step for regulated AI labs. Score 1.\n\nacceptability (1-4): socially legally politically acceptable.\nCommon strategies that violate no norms =4. This is acceptable. Score 4.\n\nEvidence: Scoping work to legal requirements is socially, legally and politically unobjectionable and aligns with regulatory expectations. Score 4.\n\nimplementability (1-4): degree to which idea can be easily implemented.\nEasy to implement at low cost, or non-radical changes =4? Requires comprehensive review, legal-scientist collaboration monthly. Some changes but reasonably feasible. Could be 3 = some changes, or reasonably feasible measures. Easy? Might need effort. I'd say 3.\n\nEvidence: Conducting a project audit and monthly mapping meetings requires coordination but uses existing staff and processes at modest cost. Score 3.\n\nReturn format: echo id exactly. For each subscale give <subscale>_evidence then <subscale>.\n\nNeed order: effectiveness, applicability, paradigm_relatedness, originality, acceptability, implementability\n\nProvide output.\n\n",
