g logs, not creating them. Hmm.\n\nMaybe applicability = 3? Solves implied problem related: making logs manageable. Let's think: The mandate requires technically allow automatic recording. Proposal is about analyzing logs. That's downstream. Could be still applicable. I'd say 3 = solves an implied problem that is related to stated problem (do X to get implied Y, which applies). Implied Y = efficient compliance monitoring.\n\nI'll pick applicability 3? Or 4? The text says \"This directly addresses the regulatory need for post-market monitoring\". Post-market monitoring is part of mandate. So maybe 4.\n\nLet's decide applicability = 4. Evidence: Proposal builds a log analysis dashboard to trigger alerts for risk thresholds, which directly targets Article 12's requirement to enable logging for post-market monitoring and risk identification.\n\nEffectiveness: degree to which idea will solve problem. Reasonable and will solve stated problem without regard for workability? It would help but not ensure logs exist. So partial solution. Level 3 = reasonable and will contribute to solution of problem (it helps, but only partial). Could be 4 if we assume logs already exist. The mandate is automatic recording. Dashboard doesn't create recording. So partial.\n\nEvidence: Even with perfect anomaly detection, the lab would still need compliant logging infrastructure first, so dashboard alone does not fully meet Article 12's recording obligation.\n\nScore 3.\n\nOriginality: degree to which idea is not only rare but also ingenious. Monitoring agent for logs, real-time observability platform... This is common in ML ops. So common, mundane. Originality 1 = common, mundane, boring.\n\nEvidence: Real-time log anomaly dashboards and observability agents are standard MLOps practices widely used in AI labs today.\n\nParadigm_relatedness: degree to which idea preserves or modifies paradigm. Standard compliance and logging practice; lab's way of working unchanged? It introduces new tools but