e, new partners or offerings born from compliance)\n2 = slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements (tools, formats, processes) but still treats compliance as logging practice\n1 = paradigm preserving: standard compliance and logging practice; lab's way of working unchanged\n\nSimulation suite introduces new element tools/process but still treats compliance as logging practice. So 2 = slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements but still treats compliance as logging practice.\n\nThus scores:\nacceptability 4\nimplementability 2 (or 3)\napplicability 4\neffectiveness 3\noriginality 3\nparadigm_relatedness 2\n\nProvide evidence sentences.\n\nNeed format: echo id exactly. For each subscale give <subscale>_evidence then <subscale>.\n\nProbably output JSON? Instruction says return rating... give ... Probably bullet list.\n\nI'll produce:\n\nid: P1554\nacceptability_evidence: ...\nacceptability: 4\n...\n\nEnsure one sentence evidence.\n\nLet's craft sentences.\n\nAcceptability evidence: The simulation suite involves internal QA testing of logs and does not involve illegal data handling or offensive practices. => score 4\n\nImplementability evidence: Creating a dedicated simulation environment and quarterly risk-trigger protocols for every active high-risk system requires substantial engineering, QA resources and ongoing maintenance. => score 2\n\nApplicability evidence: Verifying that automatic logging captures Article 79(1) risks and substantial modifications directly addresses the mandate to meet Article 12 traceability requirements. => score 4\n\nEffectiveness evidence: If implemented, the suite would provide empirical proof of traceability and support post-market monitoring, but it tests rather than installs the required logging capabilities. => score 3\n\nOriginality evidence: Quarterly failure simulations specifically to audit log capture is unusual for R&D labs and shows some imaginative quality assurance thinking. => score 3\n\nParadigm_related