te to solution\". The proposal logs inputs outputs timestamps but Article 12 requires identifying situations risk, post-market monitoring, etc., and for biometric systems specific fields. Might be insufficient alone. So contributes but not full solve. So effectiveness =3.\n\nAcceptability: degree socially legally politically acceptable. Common strategies that violate no norms? Implementing logging middleware is standard compliance, common. Score 4.\n\nImplementability: degree can be easily implemented. \"some changes, or reasonably feasible measures\" vs easy low cost? Sidecar/decorator pattern within internal Python libraries to capture event data without modifying core model logic. That's some changes, reasonably feasible but not trivially easy. Probably 3 = some changes, reasonably feasible measures. Could be 4 = easy to implement at low cost or non-radical changes. Might be moderate engineering effort. I'd say 3.\n\nOriginality: degree rare ingenious imaginative surprising. Unified telemetry middleware layer with interceptor sidecar decorator is common pattern. Not expressed before? It's common mundane. Score 1 = common mundane boring.\n\nParadigm_relatedness: degree preserves or modifies paradigm anchored to problem. Proposal introduces new elements tools but still treats compliance as logging practice. That's slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements (tools, formats, processes) but still treats compliance as logging practice. Score 2.\n\nCheck definition: 4 paradigm breaking introduces new elements AND changes relationship between lab's R&D and its compliance obligations, or addresses larger problem of what lab researches. No.\n3 paradigm stretching changes that relationship (e.g., logging woven into science, new partners...). Not.\n2 slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements (tools, formats, processes) but still treats compliance as logging practice. Yes.\n\nSo outputs.\n\nNeed format: echo id exactly. For each subscale give <subscale>