cle 12(3)(d) and is a standard accountability measure that does not violate norms.\n\nParadigm_relatedness: 4 = paradigm breaking: introduces new elements AND changes relationship between lab's R&D and compliance obligations, or addresses larger problem of what lab researches. 3 = paradigm stretching: changes that relationship (e.g., logging woven into science, new partners...). 2 = slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements but still treats compliance as logging practice. 1 = paradigm preserving: standard compliance and logging practice; lab way of working unchanged.\n\nThis is basically implementing required logging for human verification. It's standard compliance logging practice. Possibly slightly paradigm stretching? It introduces digital ledger system. But still treats compliance as logging practice. Probably paradigm preserving: standard compliance. The proposal is just meeting Article 12(3)(d). So 1.\n\nEvidence: The proposal adds a sign-off log for verifiers but keeps R&D and compliance as separate logging tasks without altering research paradigm.\n\nOriginality: 4 = not expressed before AND ingenious... 3 = unusual interesting shows some imagination. 2 = interesting. 1 = common mundane boring.\n\nDigital ledger for human verification is fairly common, mundane. So 1? Could be 2 = interesting. Hmm. Recording who verified is straightforward. Common compliance idea. I'd say 1 = common, mundane, boring.\n\nEvidence: Mandatory digital sign-off with user ID and timestamp logging for human-in-the-loop verification is a common, routine compliance implementation.\n\nEffectiveness: 4 = reasonable and will solve stated problem without regard for workability (if you could do it, it would solve main problem). 3 = reasonable and will contribute to solution (helps but partial). 2 = unreasonable or unlikely to solve. 1 = solves unrelated problem.\n\nStated problem: AI R&D lab must decide how to meet logging mandate and/or adapt R&D accordingly. Approval means committing scarce implementation resources to pursue proposal, not funding to explore.\n\nProposal addresses specific identification requirement of Article 12(3)(d) for Annex III systems. The logging mandate includes many elements (period of use, reference DB, input data match, identification of persons). This only covers one sub-point. So it contributes partially. Effectiveness = 3? Will solve stated problem without regard for workability? Main problem is meet logging mandate overall. This only covers human verification part. Might be partial solution. So 3 = reasonable and will contribute to solution.\n\nEvidence: The protocol satisfies Article 12(3)(d) identification of verifiers but does not address other required logs such as use periods, reference databases, or input matches.\n\nApplicability: 4 = solves an identified problem that is directly related to stated problem (do X to get Y, and Y is part of stated problem). 3 = solves implied problem related. 2 = may have some benefit within special situation... 1 = intervention not stated...\n\nThe proposal directly applies to logging mandate for Annex III systems human verification. So 4.\n\nEvidence: The digital sign-off directly produces the verifier identification logs required by Article 12(3)(d) for high-risk AI systems.\n\nReturn rating echoing id exactly. Format? Probably JSON or lines. Instruction says give <subscale>_evidence then <subscale>. Let's produce structured output.\n\nProvide id P2496 then each evidence and score.\n\nProbably output as list.\n\nI'll output:\n\nid: P2496\nimplementability_evidence: ...\nimplementability: 3\netc.\n\nEnsure one sentence evidence.\n\nLet's craft sentences concise.\n\nImplementability evidence: Integrating a mandatory digital sign-off with automatic user ID and timestamp logging into the existing model evaluation UI requires modest UI and authentication changes that are reasonably feasible.\n\nScore 3.\n\nAcceptability evidence: Recording verifier identity and timestamps is explicitly required by Article 12(3)(d) and represents a common accountability practice that violates no norms.\n\nScore 4.\n\nParadigm_relatedness evidence: The proposal implements standard compliance logging for human verification without changing how the lab conducts R&D or its relationship to compliance.\n\nScore 1.\n\nOriginality evidence: A digital ledger for mandatory sign-off of human verifiers is a routine, widely used compliance control rather than an ingenious or surprising idea.\n\nScore 1.\n\nEffectiveness evidence: The protocol will meet the specific identification requirement in Article 12(3)(d) but leaves other Article 12 logging obligations unaddressed, so it only partially solves the mandate.\n\nScore 3.\n\nApplicability evidence: The sign-off system directly produces the verifier identification logs mandated for Annex III high-risk systems under Article 12(3)(d).\n\nScore 4.\n\nReturn.",
