resses meeting logging mandate. I'd rate 4.\n\nimplementability (1-4): degree to which idea can be easily implemented.\n4 = easy low cost or non-radical changes\n3 = some changes, or reasonably feasible measures\n2 = significant change or expensive/difficult\n1 = totally infeasible\n\nCreating specialized compliance smoke tests in CI/CD is reasonably feasible. Some changes. Maybe 3. Could be 3.\n\nacceptability (1-4): degree to which idea is socially legally politically acceptable.\n4 = common strategies that violate no norms\n3 = somewhat uncommon but do not offend\nProposal is standard testing. Acceptable. 4.\n\nNeed format: echo id, for each subscale give <subscale>_evidence sentence then <subscale> integer.\n\nOutput presumably JSON? Instruction says return rating... give \"<subscale>_evidence\" first and then \"<subscale>\". Probably produce list.\n\nWe'll output:\n\nid: P1839\nparadigm_relatedness_evidence: ...\nparadigm_relatedness: 2\n...\n\nLet's craft one sentence evidence each.\n\nparadigm_relatedness_evidence: It introduces CI/CD smoke tests for logs but keeps compliance as a logging practice without changing the lab’s R&D-compliance relationship.\nparadigm_relatedness: 2\n\noriginality_evidence: Treating Article 12 logging as a testable functional requirement with automated checks is an interesting but increasingly common software engineering practice.\noriginality: 2\n\napplicability_evidence: The test suite directly targets the mandate to technically enable automatic Article 12 logging and prevent compliance regressions during development.\napplicability: 4\n\neffectiveness_evidence: If implemented, the framework would ensure required log triggers are captured, thereby solving the core logging-mandate implementation problem.\neffectiveness: 4\n\nimplementability_evidence: Building compliance smoke tests into an existing CI/CD pipeline requires some engineering effort but is reasonably feasible for an R&D lab.\nimplementability: 3\n\nacceptability_evid