ensive difficult but not impossible =2; totally infeasible =1\n\nGraph-based tracking system records every mutation in training pipeline as traceable event. That's significant change? Implementing version-controlled metadata linking hyperparameters dataset snapshots to performance is feasible but requires engineering. Probably some changes, reasonably feasible measures => 3. Could be 2 significant change expensive/difficult. I'd say 3.\n\nEffectiveness (1-4): degree will solve problem.\n4 = reasonable and will solve stated problem without regard for workability\n3 = reasonable and will contribute to solution (partial)\n2 = unreasonable or unlikely to solve problem\n1 = solves unrelated problem\n\nProblem: decide how to meet logging mandate and/or adapt R&D accordingly. Approval means committing scarce implementation resources to pursue proposal, not funding to explore whether idea might someday work.\n\nProposal aims to generate technical documentation through version-controlled metadata, link hyperparameters etc, record every mutation as traceable event, export human-readable reports for regulatory review. Does this meet Article 12 logging mandate? Article 12 requires automatic recording of events logs over lifetime, recording period of each use, reference database, input data for match, identification of natural persons. The proposal focuses on model iterations provenance, not necessarily system use logs post-deployment. It addresses technical documentation and training pipeline traceability. Might contribute but not fully solve logging mandate for high-risk AI systems in operation. Effectiveness maybe 3 = contributes to solution, partial.\n\nApplicability (1-4): degree idea clearly applies to stated problem.\n4 = solves identified problem directly related\n3 = solves implied problem related\n2 = may have some benefit within special situation and somehow relates\n1 = intervention not stated or does not produce useful outcome\n\nIt applies to record-keeping mandate