d will solve stated problem without regard for workability (if you could do it, it would solve main problem)\n3 = reasonable and will contribute to solution\nProposal is theoretical research project exploring... prototype methods. Will it solve logging mandate? Research may not guarantee solution. If successful, it would help meet logs without compromising privacy, but does not directly ensure compliance with Article 12 logging requirements which require recording events, specific data like input data for matches, identification of natural persons. Anonymized logs might conflict with requirement to record identification. So maybe effectiveness is limited.\n\nEffectiveness: reasonable and will contribute to solution? Might be partial. Zero-knowledge proofs may not satisfy regulatory requirement for traceability of natural persons. So it may not solve main problem. Could be 3 = contributes.\n\nI think effectiveness = 3.\n\nacceptability (1-4): degree to which idea is socially, legally, or politically acceptable.\n4 = common strategies that violate no norms\n3 = somewhat uncommon or unusual strategies that do not offend sensibilities\nResearch on privacy-preserving traceability is acceptable. Not offensive. Might be somewhat uncommon but acceptable. Probably 3? Or 4? Common strategies? Privacy-preserving logs are not common in labs. Maybe 3.\n\nImplementability (1-4): degree to which idea can be easily implemented.\nProposal is theoretical research project exploring cryptographic techniques, prototype on existing architectures. That's significant change, expensive/difficult but not totally impossible. Implementability = 2 ? Significant change, or expensive or difficult, but not totally impossible. Or 3 = some changes, reasonably feasible measures. Research project is resource intensive.\n\nThe problem says approval means committing scarce implementation resources to pursue a proposal, not funding to explore whether idea might someday work. So theoretical research is maybe not easily implementable? Might be 2 = significant change, expensive or difficult.\n\nAlso implementability anchored to lab's ability to meet mandate now. Theoretical research is not easy low cost. I'd give 2.\n\nLet's produce output with each subscale evidence sentence then score.\n\nNeed format: echo id exactly. For each subscale in order listed, give \"<subscale>_evidence\" (one sentence) first and then \"<subscale>\" (integer).\n\nProbably JSON? Return rating. Probably list.\n\nI'll output:\n\nid: P1887\noriginality_evidence: ...\noriginality: 3\n...\n\nMake sure one sentence evidence.\n\nLet's craft sentences.\n\noriginality_evidence: The proposal combines zero-knowledge proofs and differential privacy for regulatory logs, which is unusual for an AI lab responding to Article 12 and shows imagination beyond standard logging tools. Score 3\n\nparadigm_relatedness_evidence: It reframes compliance as a research frontier on secure AI governance, changing the relationship between R&D and logging obligations rather than treating logging as mere practice. Score 4\n\napplicability_evidence: Creating verifiable but anonymized event logs directly addresses how to meet Article 12's automatic recording mandate while managing privacy concerns. Score 4? Could be 3.\n\nLet's decide final scores:\n\noriginality: 3\nparadigm_relatedness: 4\napplicability: 4? Hmm maybe 3 because solves implied problem of privacy tension not explicitly stated. Stated problem is how to meet logging mandate and/or adapt R&D. Privacy tension is implied. Could be 3.\n\nLet's read applicability definition: 4 = solves an identified problem that is directly related to the stated problem (do X to get Y, and Y is part of the stated problem)\n3 = solves an implied problem that is related to the stated problem (do X to get an implied Y, which applies to the stated problem)\n\nThe stated problem is meet logging mandate and/or adapt R&D. Privacy-preserving logs is directly about meeting mandate. So 4.\n\neffectiveness: 3\n\nacceptability: 3? Or 4? Common strategies that violate no norms. Research on privacy preserving is acceptable. Might be common? Probably 4 = common strategies that violate no norms or sensibilities. It's not violating. Maybe 4.\n\nLet's choose acceptability 4.\n\nimplementability: 2\n\nProvide output.\n\n",
