es to stated problem. Solves an identified problem directly related to stated problem (do X to get Y, and Y is part of stated problem). Yes do classification to get targeted logging compliance. That's directly related. So 4.\n\nNovelty paradigm_relatedness: degree to which idea preserves or modifies paradigm anchored to this problem. Options: 4 paradigm breaking introduces new elements AND changes relationship between lab's R&D and its compliance obligations, or addresses larger problem of what lab researches. 3 paradigm stretching changes that relationship (e.g., logging woven into science, new partners or offerings born from compliance). 2 slightly paradigm stretching introduces new elements (tools, formats, processes) but still treats compliance as logging practice. 1 paradigm preserving standard compliance and logging practice; lab's way unchanged.\n\nClassification system is standard compliance practice. It's essentially risk mapping and registry. That's standard. Slightly paradigm stretching? It introduces new elements tools/processes but still treats compliance as logging practice. Might be 2. Could be 1 paradigm preserving: standard compliance and logging practice; lab's way unchanged. Hmm cross-functional committee, living registry triggers workflows - that's somewhat process improvement but still standard.\n\nProbably paradigm_relatedness = 2 (slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements but still treats compliance as logging practice). Evidence: It adds an inventory and workflow trigger but keeps compliance as a separate administrative mapping exercise rather than changing R&D-compliance relationship.\n\nOriginality: degree to which idea is rare but also ingenious. Common, mundane, boring =1. Interesting=2. Unusual interesting shows some imagination=3. Not expressed before AND ingenious...=4.\n\nClassification/inventory system for AI Act risk is common practice. So 1 common mundane boring. Evidence: Risk-tier inventories and compliance registries