adigm breaking: introduces new elements AND changes the relationship between lab's R&D and its compliance obligations, OR addresses larger problem of what lab researches. It does both? It addresses larger problem of explainability/safety which is what lab researches. So maybe 4.\n\nBut also could be stretching. Let's interpret strictly: paradigm breaking requires introduces new elements AND changes relationship... The proposal does introduce novel probing mechanisms (new elements) and changes relationship (transforms burden into opportunity). So meets AND. So 4.\n\nEvidence sentence: The initiative recasts mandatory logging as a research program to advance explainability, thereby linking compliance directly to core R&D goals.\n\nScore 4.\n\noriginality (1-4):\n4 = not expressed before AND ingenious...\n3 = unusual, interesting; shows some imagination\n2 = interesting\n1 = common\n\nCreating interpretable logs translating latent states... novel probing mechanisms. Is this rare? Possibly unusual interesting. Might be unusual but not necessarily ingenious surprising? Could be 3. Maybe 3.\n\nEvidence: Developing human-comprehensible logs from latent decision paths is an uncommon research angle compared with standard audit-log implementations.\n\nScore 3? Or 4? Not expressed before AND ingenious... Hard to claim not expressed before. Probably unusual interesting =3.\n\nacceptability (1-4):\n4 = common strategies that violate no norms\n3 = somewhat uncommon or unusual strategies that do not offend sensibilities\n2 = offends...\n1 = radically violates\n\nResearch initiative to improve interpretability is acceptable. Unusual but does not offend. So 3? Common strategies that violate no norms would be just implement logging. This is research project, somewhat uncommon. Might be 3.\n\nEvidence: A lab-led research effort to improve explainability aligns with scientific norms and regulatory expectations and raises no legal or ethical concerns.\n\nScore 4? \"common strategies tha