gies that violate no norms or sensibilities.\n\nOriginality (1-4): degree to which idea is not only rare but also ingenious...\n4 = not expressed before AND ingenious/imaginative/surprising\n3 = unusual interesting shows some imagination\n2 = interesting\n1 = common mundane boring\n\nTransforming regulatory requirement into source of high-value scientific data. This is maybe somewhat interesting but not extremely novel? Might be 3 = unusual, interesting; shows some imagination. Or 2 = interesting. Reference class: experienced evaluator would expect AI R&D labs to give responses to mandate. Using logs for drift analysis is plausible but maybe uncommon? I'd say originality = 3.\n\nParadigm_relatedness (1-4): degree to which idea preserves or modifies paradigm.\n4 = paradigm breaking: introduces new elements AND changes relationship between lab's R&D and compliance obligations, or addresses larger problem of what lab researches\n3 = paradigm stretching: changes that relationship (e.g., logging woven into science, new partners...)\n2 = slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements but still treats compliance as logging practice\n1 = paradigm preserving: standard compliance\n\nThis proposal transforms requirement into source of scientific data, weaving logging into science. That sounds like paradigm stretching: changes relationship between R&D and compliance. Maybe 3. Could be 4? Does it introduce new elements AND change relationship? It does change relationship. Does it address larger problem of what lab researches? Might be about long-term model stability. Possibly paradigm stretching =3.\n\nLet's pick:\neffectiveness_evidence: The project assumes logs already exist and focuses on analysis rather than building the mandated logging capabilities.\neffectiveness = 3? Wait evidence says assumes logs exist, so doesn't solve mandate. That suggests effectiveness lower. Maybe effectiveness =2? Reasonable? Hmm.\n\nAlternative interpretation: Problem is how to meet logging mandate and/or adapt R&D accordingly. The proposal addresses adapt R&D accordingly part. So it contributes partially. So effectiveness 3.\n\nApplicability evidence: It directly applies logs required by Article 12 to a research use case relevant to R&D adaptation.\nApplicability =4? \"solves an identified problem that is directly related to stated problem (do X to get Y, and Y is part of stated problem)\". Identified problem includes adapt R&D accordingly. Yes.\n\nMaybe applicability 4.\n\nImplementability evidence: It requires existing logs and data science expertise but no new regulatory infrastructure.\nimplementability =3? Or 4 easy low cost? Advanced statistical anomaly detection may be some changes. Let's say 3.\n\nAcceptability evidence: Using mandated logs for internal research complies with traceability goals and raises no legal or ethical concerns.\nacceptability =4\n\nOriginality evidence: Repurposing compliance logs for longitudinal drift research is unusual among typical compliance responses.\noriginality =3\n\nParadigm_relatedness evidence: It weaves logging into scientific inquiry, shifting compliance from overhead to research input.\nparadigm_relatedness =3\n\nReturn JSON? Format: For each subscale give \"<subscale>_evidence\" then \"<subscale>\". Probably output lines.\n\nWill produce:\n\nid P1743\neffectiveness_evidence ...\neffectiveness 3\n...\n\nLet's output.\n\n",
