ard. I'd say 1 = common, mundane, boring. Or 2 = interesting? The description is generic. Likely 1.\n\nEffectiveness (1-4): degree will solve problem.\n4 = reasonable and will solve stated problem without regard for workability\n3 = reasonable and will contribute...\n2 = unreasonable or unlikely...\n1 = solves unrelated problem\n\nWill automated traceability framework meet logging mandate? Captures input-output pairs, timestamps, reference DB IDs. Article 12 requires logs of events relevant for identifying risk, post-market monitoring, etc., and for Annex III point1(a) specific items: period of use, reference database, input data for match, identification of natural persons... It captures some but maybe not all. Still reasonable will solve. Could be 4? Without regard workability, it would solve main problem of meeting logging mandate and adapt R&D. Might be partial? It says ensures legal compliance without adding manual overhead. I'd say 4 = reasonable and will solve stated problem.\n\nApplicability (1-4): degree idea clearly applies to stated problem.\n4 = solves identified problem directly related\n3 = solves implied problem...\nProposal directly addresses logging mandate. So 4.\n\nAcceptability (1-4): socially, legally, politically acceptable.\n4 = common strategies that violate no norms\nLogging middleware is acceptable. 4.\n\nImplementability (1-4): degree can be easily implemented.\n4 = easy to implement at low cost, or non-radical changes\n3 = some changes, or reasonably feasible measures\n2 = significant change, or expensive/difficult\nMiddleware integration into deployment pipelines - maybe some changes. Could be reasonably feasible. Might be 3. Not trivially easy. It says without adding significant manual documentation overhead to researchers. Implementing middleware is moderate effort. I'd say 3 = some changes, reasonably feasible.\n\nCheck ordering: paradigm_relatedness evidence sentence then score. Then originality evidence then score... etc.\n\nNeed ou