artial)\n\nIt contributes but not full logging mandate. So 3.\n\nEvidence: It addresses the verifier-identification logging requirement but does not cover the broader automatic event logging, risk-trigger recording, or system-wide traceability mandated by Article 12(1)-(2).\n\nScore 3.\n\nimplementability (1-4): degree to which idea can be easily implemented.\nEasy to implement at low cost, or non-radical changes? Digital signature system linked to internal identity provider integrated into final validation stage. Some changes, reasonably feasible measures? Might be some changes. Implementability 3 = some changes, or reasonably feasible measures. Could be 4? Easy to implement at low cost? Digital signature integration may require development. I'd say 3.\n\nEvidence: Implementing a digital signature workflow tied to an existing identity provider requires modest engineering changes to the validation stage but is technically feasible with current tools.\n\nScore 3.\n\nacceptability (1-4): degree to which idea is socially, legally, or politically acceptable.\nCommon strategies that violate no norms? Logging who verified is legally required, socially acceptable. Common strategies that violate no norms =4.\n\nEvidence: Logging verifier identity via digital signatures aligns with legal mandates and standard accountability practices without offending sensibilities.\n\nScore 4.\n\nparadigm_relatedness (1-4): degree to which idea preserves or modifies paradigm.\n4 = paradigm breaking: introduces new elements AND changes relationship between lab's R&D and its compliance obligations, or addresses larger problem of what lab researches\n3 = paradigm stretching: changes that relationship (e.g., logging woven into science, new partners...)\n2 = slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements (tools, formats, processes) but still treats compliance as logging practice\n1 = paradigm preserving: standard compliance and logging practice; lab's way unchanged\n\nProposal is formalized workflow for documenting verification role. Introduces new elements but still treats compliance as logging practice. Slightly paradigm stretching? It integrates into final validation stage of prototypes. Might be slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements but still treats compliance as logging practice. That's level 2.\n\nEvidence: The protocol adds a documentation tool to the validation stage but retains compliance as an add-on logging practice rather than reshaping R&D.\n\nScore 2.\n\noriginality (1-4): degree to which idea is not only rare but also ingenious...\n4 = not expressed before AND ingenious\n3 = unusual, interesting; shows some imagination\n2 = interesting\n1 = common, mundane, boring\n\nHuman-in-the-loop verification audit with digital signatures for AI Act compliance seems common/mundane. Probably 1 or 2. Common compliance practice. I'd say 1 = common, mundane, boring.\n\nEvidence: Digitally signed human verification logs are a common, mundane compliance measure widely expected for regulated AI systems.\n\nScore 1.\n\nReturn rating echoing id.\n\nNeed format: For each subscale give <subscale>_evidence then <subscale>.\n\nLet's output structured.\n\n",
