ls into immutable DB. That's significant engineering effort, but feasible. Might be 3 = some changes reasonably feasible. Could be 2? Probably 3.\n\nacceptability: socially legally politically acceptable.\n4 common strategies violate no norms\n3 somewhat uncommon unusual strategies that do not offend sensibilities\n2 offends sensibilities somewhat\n1 radically violates\n\nLogging is required, immutable DB is acceptable. So 4.\n\napplicability: degree clearly applies to stated problem.\n4 solves identified problem directly related\n3 solves implied problem related\nProposal meets logging mandate and adapt R&D. Yes directly applies. Score 4.\n\neffectiveness: degree will solve problem.\n4 reasonable and will solve stated problem without regard workability\n3 reasonable and will contribute to solution (partial)\nWill ensure total traceability? It captures inputs outputs timestamps, but Article 12 requires specific events for risk etc. Might be partial. But overall decouples and ensures traceability. Probably 3? Reasonable and will contribute but not fully guarantee compliance with all logging specifics like reference database, natural persons identification. It says capture system inputs outputs timestamps across all versions. Could be sufficient. Maybe effectiveness 3 = contributes.\n\nBut maybe 4: if you could do it, it would solve main problem. It says ensuring total traceability. I'd give 3 to be safe.\n\nReturn JSON? The instruction says return rating echoing its id exactly. For each subscale give \"<subscale>_evidence\" then \"<subscale>\". Probably output lines.\n\nWe'll produce:\n\nid: P2837\noriginality_evidence: ... \noriginality: 2\netc.\n\nLet's produce clean format.\n\n",
