ot extremely novel? Compressed logging formats, selective event triggering, efficient metadata indexing are known. Might be interesting. Probably 2 = interesting. Could be 3 = unusual interesting shows some imagination. The reference class: experienced evaluator would expect AI R&D labs to give responses like... Maybe optimizing logging is common. I'd say 2 (interesting). Could be 3? Hmm.\n\nLet's choose originality = 2? Might be 3. Let's think: \"transforms regulatory requirement into optimization problem\" is a framing but research into low-overhead logging is fairly standard. Not ingenious. So 2 = interesting.\n\nacceptability (1-4):\n4 = common strategies that violate no norms\n3 = somewhat uncommon or unusual strategies that do not offend sensibilities\n2 = offends sensibilities somewhat but not totally unacceptable\n1 = radically violates laws...\n\nResearch project is acceptable. Common strategies? Research is acceptable. Probably 4 = common strategies that violate no norms. Or 3? It's research, acceptable. I'd say 4.\n\nimplementability (1-4):\n4 = easy to implement at low cost, or non-radical changes\n3 = some changes, or reasonably feasible measures\n2 = significant change, or expensive or difficult, but not totally impossible\n1 = totally infeasible\n\nDedicated research project aimed at minimizing costs... That's a research project, requires scarce implementation resources. Approval means committing resources to pursue proposal, not funding to explore whether idea might someday work. Wait statement: Approval in this lab means committing scarce implementation resources to pursue a proposal, not funding to explore whether an idea might someday work.\n\nSo proposal is a research project to investigate... That is exploration, not implementation. That may be difficult? Implementability = degree can be easily implemented. A dedicated research project is significant change, expensive or difficult. Might be 2? Or 3? Some changes reasonably feasible. Research project is feasible but requires resources. The problem statement: must decide how to meet logging mandate and/or adapt R&D accordingly. Approval means committing scarce implementation resources to pursue proposal, not funding to explore whether idea might someday work.\n\nSo proposing a research project is actually not meeting the mandate now; it's exploring. That may be less implementable? Hmm implementability refers to can be easily implemented. A research project is not trivial but reasonably feasible. Probably 3 = some changes, or reasonably feasible measures. I'd say 3.\n\neffectiveness (1-4):\n4 = reasonable and will solve stated problem without regard for workability\n3 = reasonable and will contribute to solution (partial)\n2 = unreasonable or unlikely to solve problem\n1 = solves unrelated problem\n\nWill low-overhead traceability mechanisms solve the logging mandate? It would help meet mandate with less cost, but it's research not immediate solution. If you could do it, it would solve main problem? Maybe partially. It addresses computational/storage costs, but mandate requires logging. Minimizing overhead helps compliance but doesn't guarantee compliance. Might be partial. Probably 3 = reasonable and will contribute to solution of problem (helps but partial). Could be 4? \"will solve stated problem without regard for workability\" If research succeeded, it would solve cost issue, but mandate is to log, not just low overhead. Might still solve. Hmm.\n\nI think effectiveness = 3 (contributes). Because it doesn't guarantee compliance immediately.\n\napplicability (1-4):\n4 = solves an identified problem that is directly related to stated problem\n3 = solves implied problem related to stated problem\n2 = may have some benefit within special situation and somehow relates\n1 = not stated\n\nProposal clearly applies to logging mandate. It solves cost of logging which is directly related. So 4.\n\nNow need output format: echo id exactly. For each subscale in order l