nts (tools, formats, processes) but still treats compliance as logging practice\n1 = paradigm preserving: standard compliance and logging practice; lab's way unchanged\n\nAutomated pipeline extracts metadata to auto-generate reports. Introduces new tool but still treats compliance as logging practice. Slightly paradigm stretching? It introduces tools but doesn't change relationship? It reduces burden, maybe still compliance separate. I'd say 2 = slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements but still treats compliance as logging practice.\n\nOriginality (1-4): degree rare but ingenious...\n4 = not expressed before AND ingenious\n3 = unusual, interesting; shows some imagination\n2 = interesting\n1 = common, mundane, boring\n\nAutomated doc pipeline from experiment trackers is fairly common idea. Might be common, mundane. Possibly 1 or 2. I'd say interesting but not rare. Many labs do auto reports. Could be 2 = interesting. Or 1 = common. Hmm. Auto-generate compliance reports using MLflow/Git is somewhat common practice. Maybe 2? Let's pick 2 = interesting.\n\nRelevance\napplicability (1-4): degree clearly applies to stated problem.\n4 = solves identified problem directly related\n3 = solves implied problem related\n2 = may have some benefit within special situation\n1 = not stated...\n\nStated problem: decide how to meet logging mandate and/or adapt R&D. Proposal is about auto-generate required compliance reports, not specifically logging? Article 12 is record-keeping/logs. Technical documentation pipeline addresses Recital 71 technical documentation, but proposal text says compile system characteristics and validation results into report. That's documentation, not logging events. But still related to compliance. Applies to problem? Might be applicable but maybe addresses documentation not logging. The mandate includes logging. Auto-generate reports reduces administrative burden. Might be partially applicable.\n\nApplies to stated problem? Problem is how to meet logging mandate. Proposal focuses on technical documentation pipeline. Could be 3 = solves implied problem related (administrative burden). Or 4 = solves identified problem directly related? Logging mandate requires automatic recording of events logs. This proposal extracts metadata from experiment trackers... Not clearly logs. Hmm.\n\nProbably applicability 3: solves implied problem that is related (administrative burden for compliance). Might be 3.\n\nEffectiveness (1-4): degree will solve problem.\n4 = reasonable and will solve stated problem without regard workability\n3 = reasonable and will contribute to solution (partial)\n2 = unreasonable/unlikely\n1 = solves unrelated\n\nAuto documentation reduces admin burden but does not ensure automatic recording of events logs over lifetime per Article 12. So partial contribution, not solve logging mandate. Effectiveness maybe 3? Reasonable and will contribute to solution of problem (it helps). Might be 3.\n\nAlternatively effectiveness 2? It probably will not solve logging. The proposal is about technical documentation pipeline, not logging events. So unlikely to solve main problem. Could be 2 = unreasonable or unlikely to solve problem.\n\nThe stated problem: how to meet logging mandate and/or adapt R&D accordingly. Auto doc pipeline addresses compliance reports, not logging events. Might be tangential. I think effectiveness 3 is reasonable contribution to compliance overall but not logging specifically. Hmm.\n\nLet's decide:\n\nacceptability_evidence: The tool automates internal documentation using existing experiment trackers without violating legal or social norms.\nacceptability: 4\n\nimplementability_evidence: Integration with existing Git and MLflow requires scripting and workflow changes that are feasible for an R&D lab.\nimplementability: 3\n\nparadigm_relatedness_evidence: It adds automation tools to compliance paperwork but keeps compliance as a separate administrative practice from R&D.\nparadigm_relatedness: 2\n\noriginality_evidence: Auto-generating reports from version control and experiment metadata is a familiar DevOps/MLOps practice rather than an imaginative novelty.\noriginality: 2? Could be 1. Let's pick 1? Common, mundane. Maybe 1 = common mundane boring. I think auto doc pipeline is common. Let's choose 1.\n\noriginality: 1\n\napplicability_evidence: The pipeline directly addresses the administrative burden of producing compliance documentation required by the AI Act for high-risk systems.\napplicability: 3? Or 4? Solves identified problem directly related? The stated problem is logging mandate. Documentation is related but not same. I'd say 3.\n\neffectiveness_evidence: While it reduces manual paperwork, it does not create the automatic event logging over system lifetime mandated by Article 12.\neffectiveness: 2? Unlikely to solve logging problem. Might be 3 contributes. Let's pick 2 = unreasonable or unlikely to solve problem (it probably will not work for logging). Actually it could contribute partially to compliance overall. Hmm.\n\nGiven instrument separates applicability and effectiveness. Applicability is clear applies. Effectiveness low. Maybe effectiveness 2.\n\nLet's output.\n\nNeed format: echo id exactly. For each subscale in order listed, give \"<subscale>_evidence\" one sentence then \"<subscale>\" integer.\n\nProbably JSON output.\n\nReturn rating of this proposal, echoing its id exactly. For each subscale... give ... first and then ...\n\nProvide as lines.\n\nI'll produce JSON with keys.\n\nLet's do:\n\nid: P1294\nacceptability_evidence: ...\nacceptability: 4\n...\n\nEnsure one sentence evidence.\n\nProceed.",
