w elements AND changes relationship between lab's R&D and compliance obligations, or addresses larger problem of what lab researches.\nThis is paradigm stretching? Changes relationship e.g., logging woven into science, new partners/offers born from compliance. It positions lab as leader in transparent AI, transforms legal burden into market advantage. That's changes relationship, creates new offering. Possibly 3 = paradigm stretching: changes that relationship.\n\nIt introduces tools but still treats compliance as logging practice? Actually it says transforms burden into competitive advantage, positioning as leader. That is change relationship. Could be 3.\n\nOriginality (1-4): degree to which idea is rare and ingenious...\nAutomated traceability dashboards for AI compliance is unusual? Probably common/mundane? Many companies have compliance dashboards. Might be interesting but not ingenious. Score 2 = interesting.\n\nEvidence: Compliance dashboards mapping logs to regulation are a familiar industry practice rather than an imaginative novelty.\n\nimplementability (1-4): degree to which idea can be easily implemented.\nSome changes, reasonably feasible measures. Developing visualization tools and automated reporting scripts is some changes, reasonably feasible. Score 3.\n\nEvidence: Building dashboards and scripts requires software engineering effort but uses existing log data and standard visualization stacks, making it reasonably feasible.\n\nacceptability (1-4): degree to which idea is socially, legally, politically acceptable.\nCommon strategies that violate no norms. Score 4.\n\nEvidence: Creating transparent reporting tools aligns with regulatory expectations and does not offend legal or social sensibilities.\n\nNeed format: For each subscale give <subscale>_evidence then <subscale>. Probably JSON? Return rating echoing id. Likely output list.\n\nProvide in order: applicability_evidence, applicability, effectiveness_evidence, effectiveness, paradigm_relatedness