toring, monitoring operation, and for point 1(a) Annex III specific items like period of use, reference database, input data match, identification of natural persons. Framework provides hooks for timestamps, input tracking, user identification. Probably will contribute but maybe not fully cover all specifics? It says pre-built hooks for timestamps, input tracking, user identification. That covers many but maybe not reference database etc. However effectiveness rating: reasonable and will solve stated problem without regard for workability (if you could do it, it would solve main problem). Might be 4. Could argue partial solution: contributes. Hmm.\n\nIt says standardized library for automated event logging across all high-risk AI projects, pre-built hooks for timestamps, input tracking, user identification. Pipes logs into existing secure storage. Minimizes per-project burden while ensuring consistent regulatory compliance. Seems it would solve logging mandate. I'd rate 4 = reasonable and will solve stated problem without regard for workability.\n\nEvidence: If implemented, the library would automate the required event logging and traceability across projects, directly meeting Article 12's mandate. Score 4.\n\nacceptability (1-4): degree to which idea is socially, legally, politically acceptable.\nCommon strategies that violate no norms or sensibilities =4. It's standard compliance software library. Acceptable. Evidence: Building internal logging libraries for regulatory compliance is a common, norm-conforming engineering practice. Score 4.\n\nimplementability (1-4): degree to which idea can be easily implemented.\nEasy to implement at low cost, or non-radical changes? Some changes, or reasonably feasible measures =3? Engineering team develop API wrappers research scientists integrate with minimal code changes. Pipes into existing secure storage. That's some changes, reasonably feasible. Not trivially easy? Probably 3. Could be 4 easy? Developing standardized library across all high-risk projects is not low cost trivial; requires engineering effort. So 3 = some changes, or reasonably feasible measures.\n\nEvidence: Developing a shared library and requiring integration into training and inference loops requires coordinated engineering work but uses existing storage. Score 3.\n\nparadigm_relatedness (1-4): degree to which idea preserves or modifies paradigm.\n4 = paradigm breaking: introduces new elements AND changes relationship between lab's R&D and its compliance obligations, or addresses larger problem of what lab researches\n3 = paradigm stretching: changes that relationship (e.g., logging woven into science, new partners or offerings born from compliance)\n2 = slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements (tools, formats, processes) but still treats compliance as logging practice\n1 = paradigm preserving: standard compliance and logging practice; lab's way of working is unchanged\n\nThis proposal is a standardized software library for logging. That's introducing new tool but still treats compliance as logging practice. Slightly paradigm stretching? It introduces new elements (tools, formats, processes) but still treats compliance as logging practice. That's definition of 2. It doesn't change relationship between R&D and compliance obligations fundamentally; just makes logging easier. So 2.\n\nEvidence: The framework adds a reusable logging tool but keeps compliance as an add-on logging practice rather than reshaping R&D. Score 2.\n\noriginality (1-4): degree to which idea is not only rare but also ingenious...\n4 = not expressed before AND ingenious...\n3 = unusual, interesting; shows some imagination\n2 = interesting\n1 = common, mundane, boring\n\nUnified compliance telemetry framework is common. Software library for logging compliance is mundane. So 1 = common, mundane, boring.\n\nEvidence: Centralized logging libraries for regulatory compliance are a common, well-known engineering approach in AI labs. Score 1.\